WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.849
An agent can write a select that looks perfect.

00:00:02.849 --> 00:00:04.937
So why do its arrow keys do nothing?

00:00:09.082 --> 00:00:10.106
Hi, I'm Linnea!

00:00:10.106 --> 00:00:13.177
Vercel's UI SDK is not a generative UI runtime.

00:00:13.177 --> 00:00:17.271
It is behaviors for agents to build with, and a bridge for

00:00:17.271 --> 00:00:18.977
agents to use the result.

00:00:20.800 --> 00:00:21.623
Chapter one.

00:00:21.623 --> 00:00:22.858
Behaviors, not looks.

00:00:27.467 --> 00:00:29.821
Vercel's launch video takes a select apart.

00:00:29.821 --> 00:00:31.502
Arrow keys through a list.

00:00:31.502 --> 00:00:32.847
Typing to jump ahead.

00:00:32.847 --> 00:00:33.856
Remembering the choice.

00:00:34.276 --> 00:00:37.396
Each piece is a behavior: popup, listbox, typeahead, selection.

00:00:37.396 --> 00:00:40.515
A model gets the box and the chevron right.

00:00:40.515 --> 00:00:41.902
It gets these wrong.

00:00:43.550 --> 00:00:47.493
At the bottom, a kernel: a state store, parts, and change details.

00:00:47.493 --> 00:00:51.765
On top, the behaviors, one job each, like roving focus or a popup.

00:00:52.185 --> 00:00:56.396
A primitive puts one component together: its state, the API that changes

00:00:56.396 --> 00:00:57.799
it, and its parts.

00:00:57.799 --> 00:00:59.203
No markup, no styles.

00:00:59.623 --> 00:01:03.485
Adapters render it in React, Vue, Svelte, Solid or web components.

00:01:03.485 --> 00:01:05.591
You write the markup and styles.

00:01:06.011 --> 00:01:09.859
The shipped components use the same behaviors, so a component an agent

00:01:09.859 --> 00:01:11.783
writes gets the same keyboard support.

00:01:13.800 --> 00:01:17.527
The README's search field has a name and one piece of state, the value.

00:01:17.947 --> 00:01:20.410
The API is the only way that state changes.

00:01:20.410 --> 00:01:22.599
It is what the agent will call later.

00:01:23.019 --> 00:01:24.731
The clear button is a slot.

00:01:24.731 --> 00:01:28.439
It exists only while there is text, and pressing it empties the value.

00:01:30.217 --> 00:01:34.600
The intended workflow: ask for a teammate picker, and the agent composes

00:01:34.600 --> 00:01:36.426
four behaviors into one primitive.

00:01:38.008 --> 00:01:38.726
Chapter two.

00:01:38.726 --> 00:01:39.803
Driving the page.

00:01:44.133 --> 00:01:46.485
The bridge gives a model three tools.

00:01:46.485 --> 00:01:47.830
Snapshot lists the page.

00:01:47.830 --> 00:01:48.838
Act runs steps.

00:01:48.838 --> 00:01:51.526
Query finds a component or a list item.

00:01:51.946 --> 00:01:55.910
Every component registers itself as it is made, with a ref, its

00:01:55.910 --> 00:01:58.222
accessible name, its state and its actions.

00:01:58.643 --> 00:02:00.612
Act calls the component's own API.

00:02:00.612 --> 00:02:04.878
Your change callback hears it, tagged with source agent, and can cancel it.

00:02:06.492 --> 00:02:11.872
Take the video's task: Asia Pacific, the Pro plan, three instances, preview deployments on.

00:02:11.872 --> 00:02:14.561
Snapshot first gives each control a ref.

00:02:14.981 --> 00:02:18.718
A browser agent finds each control by looking, clicks it, then

00:02:18.718 --> 00:02:20.077
reads the page again.

00:02:20.077 --> 00:02:21.096
Field after field.

00:02:21.516 --> 00:02:24.849
The bridge sends all four steps in one act call, each through

00:02:24.849 --> 00:02:25.959
the control's own API.

00:02:26.716 --> 00:02:29.923
Act returns every value, so nothing is read twice.

00:02:29.923 --> 00:02:34.199
The docs measured sixteen tool calls against five, on a different task.

00:02:35.800 --> 00:02:39.689
In the launch video, each phrase lights up as its control changes.

00:02:39.689 --> 00:02:40.661
Four settings changed.

00:02:42.717 --> 00:02:45.672
A step can name a component in words, like Jane Doe.

00:02:45.672 --> 00:02:47.283
But anyone can write page text.

00:02:47.283 --> 00:02:48.358
Is it a ref?

00:02:48.358 --> 00:02:48.627
No.

00:02:49.047 --> 00:02:52.921
Labels and row text: a checkbox labelled Jane Doe matches, and so

00:02:52.921 --> 00:02:55.180
does an Admin menu on her row.

00:02:55.180 --> 00:02:57.117
An exact label does not win.

00:02:57.537 --> 00:03:00.051
A tie narrows to components that take the action.

00:03:00.051 --> 00:03:01.728
Press passes for all of them.

00:03:02.148 --> 00:03:04.575
So the step is refused, listing both refs.

00:03:04.575 --> 00:03:06.698
The agent must pick one on purpose.

00:03:08.550 --> 00:03:09.103
Chapter three.

00:03:09.103 --> 00:03:10.484
A person in the loop.

00:03:14.525 --> 00:03:18.131
Three HTML attributes decide what an agent may do alone.

00:03:18.131 --> 00:03:19.574
People see no difference.

00:03:19.994 --> 00:03:21.255
Confirm carries a sentence.

00:03:21.255 --> 00:03:23.462
Without an allow callback the step fails.

00:03:23.462 --> 00:03:25.669
With one, it waits for the person.

00:03:26.089 --> 00:03:28.981
Hidden removes a region from what the agent can see at all.

00:03:29.401 --> 00:03:32.871
Secret lets the agent type into a field, but never read it back.

00:03:33.291 --> 00:03:36.680
And every agent change reaches your callbacks tagged as the agent's.

00:03:38.008 --> 00:03:42.366
The docs' one benchmark: browser tools on another library against the bridge.

00:03:42.366 --> 00:03:44.545
Fewer calls, less text, less time.

00:03:44.965 --> 00:03:49.292
Medians of three runs, the other library unnamed, no harness in the repo.

00:03:49.292 --> 00:03:51.622
I believe the direction, not the size.

00:03:52.858 --> 00:03:54.560
The code also shows the limits.

00:03:54.980 --> 00:03:57.147
Snapshot lists only UI SDK components.

00:03:57.147 --> 00:04:01.481
A plain button or a bare input is invisible to the agent.

00:04:01.901 --> 00:04:05.255
A failed step stops the call with no rollback, and ok can

00:04:05.255 --> 00:04:07.491
be true while a callback cancelled the change.

00:04:07.911 --> 00:04:11.100
And it is a beta, with under three weeks of commit history

00:04:11.100 --> 00:04:12.163
and three human authors.

00:04:13.350 --> 00:04:17.831
An agent driving a page by screenshots is reverse engineering an API

00:04:17.831 --> 00:04:19.325
the app already has.

00:04:19.325 --> 00:04:21.565
UI SDK hands it that API.

00:04:22.858 --> 00:04:23.581
To recap.

00:04:23.581 --> 00:04:25.390
Behaviors for agents to compose.

00:04:25.390 --> 00:04:29.007
A bridge that drives the page through its own API.

00:04:29.007 --> 00:04:31.539
And names treated as untrusted page text.

00:04:31.959 --> 00:04:34.072
Every source is in the full article.

00:04:34.072 --> 00:04:34.676
I'm Linnea.

00:04:34.676 --> 00:04:34.978
Bye!
